# GD-28 (the user, 2026-10-29; writer 10): L §2.4 "Two rules, or two items, that every key above leaves tied (two
# rows with no start, two rows read `after m`, two rows with one start, a `same` item and a row of its target and
# keys) sort by their own canonical line, compared as ASCII". Was REFCORE-UNSPECIFIED §2.4. The old comment:
# GD-24 (round 19, writer 10): "Ties L §2.4 still leaves (two rows with no start, two `after m` rows, two rows with one
# start) stay REFCORE-UNSPECIFIED where met" (faults5 item 9). L §2.4 sorts version items "... then by the start of their
# own governs span or their instant (a row with no span of its own first, then a span with no start, a row governing
# always among them, then by start; a row read after m after the dated ones of its target and keys)".
# TX-F1's text: `stmt[r1] = (...)` and `same stmt[r1] = gift`, "a same item by its left side (as an item whose
# target and keys are its left side's)": both are items of stmt[r1] with no span of their own, a tie the text leaves
# (a new site, beside the three).
=== options reprint
=== m.tally
model one
  tally 2
  on 2026-10-25
  start 2024-01-01
  end 2024-12-31

source s "made up"

unit DUK: currency minor 0.01

place acc: accounts DUK lives 2024-01-01..

place other: outside lives 2024-01-01..

input stmt: observed

input g_day: date

input g_amt: money DUK

move gift: transfers
  on g_day
  leg cash from other to acc amount g_amt

ask q: flows(acc, 2024)

version v
  cite s
  governs 2024-05
  known 2024-05-02
  covers acc
  stmt[r1] = (on: 2024-05-02, kind: transfers, leg cash from other to acc amount 5000.00 DUK) "r1"
  same stmt[r1] = gift

version plan
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2024-01-01
  g_day = 2024-05-02 "plan"
  g_amt = 5000.00 "plan"

ask q_bal: bal(acc, 2024-12-31)

ask q_x: explain bal(acc, 2024-05-02)
